            <ArticleTitle>Gradenigo's syndrome--surgical management in a child.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>393-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Otits media is a common problem. Some of its complications that were seen frequently in the preantibiotic era are rare today. We report a case of an 8 year boy who presented with earache, retro-orbital pain and diplopia secondary to a sixth nerve palsy--Gradenigo's syndrome. In this syndrome infection from the middle ear spreads medially to the petrous apex of the temporal bone. Work-up includes CT scan of the temporal bones. Timely management with intravenous antibiotics (+ surgery) is needed to prevent intra-cranial complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
